1. SingleBinary Loki (Monolithic mode)

In SingleBinary mode, all Loki components run together inside a single process, typically in one Kubernetes Pod.

For example, Loki internally contains:
- Distributor – receives incoming logs.
- Ingester – processes and stores log data.
- Querier – executes log queries.
- Query Frontend – manages and optimizes queries.
- Compactor – manages stored log data.
- Ruler – evaluates alerting rules.

In SingleBinary mode, all these components run together.

Kubernetes Cluster
       |
       v
  Loki Namespace
       |
       v
  Loki Deployment
       |
       v
  Loki Pod
       |
       +-- Distributor
       +-- Ingester
       +-- Querier
       +-- Query Frontend
       +-- Compactor
       +-- Ruler
       |
       v
  Persistent/Object Storage

You generally deploy one Loki Pod for a small installation.

Advantages:
- Easy to install and configure.
- Fewer Kubernetes resources.
- Lower infrastructure overhead.
- Easy to troubleshoot.
- Suitable for development and small environments.

Disadvantages:
- All components share CPU and memory resources.
- Cannot independently scale individual components.
- A single Pod failure can interrupt the entire Loki service when running one replica.
- Less suitable for large log volumes.


2. Distributed Loki (Microservices mode)
-----------------------------------------

In Distributed mode, Loki components run as separate Kubernetes workloads.
Instead of running everything inside one Pod, you deploy individual components.

Kubernetes Cluster
       |
       v
  Loki Namespace
       |
       +-- Distributor Pods
       |
       +-- Ingester Pods
       |
       +-- Querier Pods
       |
       +-- Query Frontend Pods
       |
       +-- Query Scheduler Pods
       |
       +-- Compactor Pods
       |
       +-- Index Gateway Pods
       |
       v
  Shared Object Storage (S3)

Each Component has its own responsiblity and can be scaled indepedently 
depending on the requiremnts

Advantages:
- Independent horizontal scaling.
- Better control over CPU and memory allocation.
- Better suited for high log ingestion volumes.
- Components can have independent availability configurations.
- Suitable for large Kubernetes environments.
Disadvantages:
- More Kubernetes resources.
- More complex configuration.
- Requires shared object storage.
- More complicated troubleshooting.
- Higher operational overhead.




Loki components and their responsibilities
1. Distributor Pods – Receive logs
- Receive logs from Grafana Alloy.
- Decide where the logs should go.
- Forward logs to Ingester Pods.
Think: Receptionist who receives and directs incoming logs.
2. Ingester Pods – Store logs
- Receive logs from Distributors.
- Organize logs into chunks.
- Compress and store the logs.
Think: Warehouse worker who organizes and stores incoming goods.
3. Querier Pods – Search logs
- Execute LogQL queries.
- Retrieve matching logs from Ingester and storage.
- Return results to Grafana.
Think: Employee who searches for the information you request.
4. Query Frontend Pods – Manage queries
- Receive queries from Grafana.
- Split large queries into smaller queries.
- Improve query performance through caching and parallel processing.
Think: Manager who divides a big task into smaller tasks.
5. Query Scheduler Pods – Schedule queries
- Manage and distribute queries among Querier Pods.
- Help prevent individual Queriers from becoming overloaded.
Think: Supervisor who distributes work among employees.
6. Compactor Pods – Clean and optimize storage
- Compact index files.
- Remove expired log data according to retention settings.
- Optimize stored index data.
Think: Maintenance worker who organizes the warehouse and removes expired goods.
7. Index Gateway Pods – Manage index access
- Provide index information to Querier Pods.
- Retrieve relevant index data from storage.
- Reduce the need for every Querier to independently handle index files.
Think: Librarian who helps employees find where information is stored.
